10. Advantages

Simple and easy to implement.

Clearly represents connections between departments.

Checks a direct connection in constant time. 

Suitable for graphs with a small or moderate number of departments.

Makes it easy to identify all direct communication links.

11. Disadvantages
Requires  memory, even when there are few links.

Adding a new department requires resizing the matrix.

It does not directly show the complete communication path between departments.

It is less memory-efficient for very large, sparsely connected networks.

12. Applications

Representing communication links between university departments.

Designing computer networks within educational institutions.

Representing connections between offices in an organization.

Modeling transport routes between locations.
Representing social networks and other connected systems.

Analyzing direct connections in network topology.

13. Conclusion

The C++ program successfully represents university departments and their communication links using an adjacency matrix. It accepts department names, stores connections, displays the matrix, and checks the connection status between two departments. This experiment helps in understanding graph representation and the practical use of adjacency matrices in network modeling.
